      "created_at": "2018-01-23T16:25:18Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-01-23T16:25:18Z",
      "body": "As a starting point, it's probably worth considering what the core system actually stores that falls under the regulation.   From what I can see, the following are the only issues:\r\n\r\nMember table: Name, Email, etc.\r\nLoginAttempt table:  IP, Email\r\n\r\nAs I understand it, the critical things we need for GDPR are statements about the use of the data, and means to expire it, etc.\r\n\r\nFor the second of the two, the simplest way to deal with it would be to take it out of the equation altogether.  If it were possible to disable the loginattempt logging with a configuration option, that solves the issue.   If the loginattempt data is needed, then some mechanism to expire/purge it periodically (maybe an add-on) would help.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
