      "created_at": "2018-01-29T02:10:34Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-01-29T02:10:34Z",
      "body": "Regarding `LoginAttempt`, there's been [recent changes](https://github.com/silverstripe/silverstripe-framework/commit/f1dd3d6f03eb1d94c29c495994a1da9176a758d9#diff-41b85a1c8dd9a73784e8b33811f10130) to only store the email hash rather than email address itself. For existing users with matching email addresses on the attempt, we're still [associating](https://github.com/silverstripe/silverstripe-framework/blob/d876e36424918909b65c0c53d4d682d50aadcf7b/src/Security/MemberAuthenticator/MemberAuthenticator.php#L165) IPs to members though, so are effectively storing personally identifable information. \r\n\r\nYou can turn off login recording via `Security.login_recording: false`. But in its default behaviour, it means that any website might need a privacy statement for CMS users, even if you're not collecting data from visitors or providing other login-based services. Maybe \"business applications\" like the CMS are exempt from GDPR, if the CMS user is employed by the \"data controller\" organisation? ",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
